[x] 3. Count the time you spend in an editor, through WakaTime's plugins

    WakaTime's editor plugins run wakatime-cli, which sends a heartbeat each
    time you edit or move around in a file. You have no ~/.wakatime.cfg, so
    pairtime makes one that has wakatime-cli send them to pairtime's agent
    on this computer, and nowhere else. It also tells wakatime-cli to read
    your AI agents' transcripts only from now on, so that it doesn't send
    the ones from before as new time.
    For VS Code, Cursor, Windsurf and other editors built on VS Code,
    install WakaTime's extension from the editor's extensions view, or with
    code --install-extension WakaTime.vscode-wakatime.
    Hack Club programs that review time through Robert read it from
    Hackatime, which this doesn't set up. If you join one, set up Hackatime
    too, and pairtime keeps working beside it.

    Saying no doesn't stop you from using pairtime.
    Without it, pairtime doesn't see the time you spend in an editor.
    You can take it out any time with pairtime uninstall, which deletes the
    files it made.

    --- /dev/null
    +++ /home/teen/.wakatime.cfg
    @@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
    +[settings]
    +api_url = http://127.0.0.1:7247/api/v1
    +api_key = 00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000a9e7
    --- /dev/null
    +++ /home/teen/.wakatime/wakatime-internal.cfg
    @@ -0,0 +1,2 @@
    +[internal]
    +ai_logs_last_parsed_at = 2026-11-24T05:30:00Z
